## 2. Database Schema (Supabase / Postgres)

Grouped by feature area. `profiles.id` is always the same UUID as the corresponding `auth.users.id` (Supabase Auth owns identity; `profiles` extends it).

### Identity

**`profiles`**

- `id` (uuid, PK, = `auth.users.id`)
- `role` (enum: `patient`, `clinician`, `receptionist` — only first two used in MVP)
- `full_name`, `phone`
- `created_at`

**`patient_details`** (1:1 with `profiles` where role = patient)

- `profile_id` (FK → profiles.id, unique)
- `date_of_birth`
- `emergency_contact_name`, `emergency_contact_phone`

_(No `clinician_details` table yet — one doctor, nothing to store beyond `profiles.full_name`. Add later if needed.)_

### Appointments & Invites

**`appointments`**

- `id` (PK)
- `patient_id` (FK → profiles.id)
- `clinician_id` (FK → profiles.id)
- `scheduled_at`
- `status` (enum: `invited`, `active`, `checked_in`, `completed`, `cancelled`)
- `created_at`

**`appointment_invites`**

- `id` (PK)
- `appointment_id` (FK → appointments.id, unique)
- `patient_email`
- `token` (unique, indexed — the magic-link secret)
- `expires_at`
- `used_at` (nullable)
- `created_at`

### Vitals

**`vitals_logs`**

- `id` (PK)
- `patient_id` (FK → profiles.id)
- `appointment_id` (FK → appointments.id, nullable — vitals can be logged anytime)
- `metric_key` (e.g. `weight`)
- `value`, `unit`
- `recorded_at`
- `source` (default `patient_manual`)

### Pre-Visit Questionnaire

**`questionnaire_templates`**

- `id` (PK)
- `question_text`
- `response_type` (MVP: `yes_no` only)
- `is_red_flag_trigger` (bool — a "Yes" here is what gets highlighted red on the clinician side)
- `sort_order`, `is_active`

**`questionnaire_responses`**

- `id` (PK)
- `appointment_id` (FK), `patient_id` (FK)
- `template_id` (FK → questionnaire_templates.id)
- `answer` (bool)
- `created_at`

### Lab Reports & Standardization — this is the HB/HGB problem

**`lab_reports`**

- `id` (PK)
- `patient_id` (FK), `appointment_id` (FK, nullable)
- `storage_path` (Supabase Storage, bucket `lab-reports`)
- `uploaded_at`
- `ocr_status` (enum: `pending`, `success`, `partial`, `failed`)
- `raw_ocr_payload` (jsonb) — the exact JSON the pipeline handed back, kept verbatim for audit/debugging
- `report_date` (nullable — the date printed on the physical report; drives the "bring the physical Oct 12th report" checklist rule)

**`lab_report_metrics`** — one row per value inside a report

- `id` (PK)
- `lab_report_id` (FK)
- `raw_key` (exactly as returned, e.g. `"HGB"`)
- `standard_key` (FK → metric_dictionary.standard_key, **nullable until matched**)
- `raw_value` (text — kept as-is since OCR text can be messy)
- `parsed_value` (numeric, nullable — cleaned once matched/converted)
- `unit_raw`, `unit_standard` (nullable)
- `confidence_score` (nullable, if the pipeline provides one)
- `needs_review` (bool, default false — true when no dictionary match or confidence is low → feeds the clinician's "AI Triage Alerts")
- `reviewed_by` (FK → profiles.id, nullable), `reviewed_value` (nullable — what the doctor manually typed in, if corrected)
- `created_at`

**`metric_dictionary`** — the actual fix for "HB / HGB → hemoglobin"

- `standard_key` (PK, e.g. `hemoglobin`)
- `display_name` (e.g. `Hemoglobin`)
- `category` (e.g. `blood`)
- `unit_standard` (e.g. `g/dL`)
- `aliases` (text array, e.g. `{HB, HGB, Hb, haemoglobin}`)
- `is_active`

This table is a normal data table, not code — so adding a new lab format your pipeline encounters later is an `UPDATE`, not a deploy. The standardization service just looks up each `raw_key` against `aliases`, sets `standard_key` + converts `unit_raw` → `unit_standard`, and flags `needs_review` when nothing matches.

### Pre-Visit Checklist

**`checklist_rule_templates`**

- `id` (PK), `label` (e.g. "Bring ID")
- `trigger_type` (enum: `always`, `on_ocr_failure`, `on_missing_report`)
- `is_active`

**`pre_visit_checklist_items`** — the actual per-appointment generated list

- `id` (PK)
- `appointment_id` (FK), `patient_id` (FK)
- `label`
- `source` (enum: `static`, `ai_generated`)
- `source_ref` (nullable uuid — e.g. the `lab_report_id` that triggered an ai_generated item)
- `is_completed`, `created_at`

### Post-Visit

**`consultation_notes`**

- `id` (PK), `appointment_id` (FK, unique), `clinician_id` (FK)
- `notes_text`, `created_at`, `updated_at`

**`prescriptions`**

- `id` (PK), `appointment_id` (FK)
- `storage_path` (bucket `prescriptions`)
- `typed_instructions` (nullable)
- `created_at`

**`post_visit_action_items`** — the "Next Steps" checklist patients see

- `id` (PK), `appointment_id` (FK), `patient_id` (FK)
- `label`, `is_completed`, `created_at`

**`quick_action_templates`** (optional convenience table)

- `id` (PK), `label` (e.g. "Prescribe Iron"), `category` (nullable), `is_active`
- Toggling one of these on the clinician side just inserts a matching row into `post_visit_action_items`.

### Storage Buckets

- `lab-reports` — patient-uploaded report photos
- `prescriptions` — doctor-uploaded prescription images

### Access notes (not full RLS policy SQL, just the shape)

- A patient should only ever see rows where `patient_id` resolves to their own `profiles.id`.
- The clinician (single doctor, MVP) can see all patients.
- `lab_reports.raw_ocr_payload` and `lab_report_metrics.raw_value`/`confidence_score` are clinician/system-only — patients don't need to see pipeline internals, just the final values once reviewed.

